# Edward's settings. ./setup.sh copies this file to .env (never commit .env)
# and fills in the four live lines below; everything else is optional and
# shown commented out with its default.
#
# Who reads it: the backend (backend/main.py loads it once running), the
# scripts launchd runs and the start scripts (scripts/lib/edward-env.sh
# parses it), and frontend/ensure-build.sh (the two NEXT_PUBLIC_* values, at
# build time). Everyone takes the process environment first and this file
# second. Nothing exports the whole file: a process's start-up environment
# can be read by every other process of your user, Edward's sandboxed shell
# included, so backend/start.sh reads only the few values it needs and
# hands the backend the ~/ paths below already expanded. Keep this file in
# the repo root (a backend/.env is not read).
#
# Many values are read on every call, so a change applies at the next turn;
# others are read once at startup. Restart the backend to be sure:
#   ./restart.sh backend
#
# Quoting: write it as bash would read it (python-dotenv and the scripts
# parse it; you may still source it by hand). Quote values with spaces
# ('Sam Rivera'), and use single quotes for anything with a $ in it (bcrypt
# hashes). A path may start with ~/ unquoted.
#
# Places where this Edward still assumes its first home are marked in the
# code with "ADAPT:" notes; docs/ADAPTING.md walks through them.

# =============================================================================
# THE ESSENTIALS (setup.sh asks for these)
# =============================================================================

# What Edward calls you, in prompts and on screen. Unset: "the owner".
EDWARD_OWNER_NAME=

# The Claude API key (console.anthropic.com > API keys). Every model call
# goes through it.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# anthropic = the Claude API (the default, also when unset). codex = the
# unofficial fallback further down.
EDWARD_LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic

# Your IANA time zone (America/Chicago, Europe/Paris ...). It seeds the
# app's own time zone setting (Settings > Locale, /settings/locale), which
# is what cron times, quiet hours and "today" use. Unset or not a zone:
# America/Chicago until the first browser visit sets the browser's zone.
TIMEZONE=

# =============================================================================
# WHO EDWARD WORKS FOR (backend/services/owner.py)
# =============================================================================

# Other names you go by, so Edward recognises you in messages.
# EDWARD_OWNER_FULL_NAME='Sam Rivera'
# EDWARD_OWNER_ALIASES=Sammy,S        # comma-separated nicknames

# Your own phone numbers, emails and Apple ID, comma-separated: how Edward
# recognises your iMessages and your self-chat ("message yourself" = a
# private line to Edward), and where imessage_self / edward_number reach
# you (EDWARD_OWNER_CHANNEL).
# EDWARD_OWNER_HANDLES=+15555550100,sam@example.com

# Your own email addresses (GMAIL_ACCOUNT counts too): mail from these is
# yours, and Edward never emails you.
# EDWARD_OWNER_EMAILS=sam@example.com

# The people at the household hub, comma-separated, you first (the hub's
# who-toggle and its themes go by position). Unset: just you. Everyone but
# you also counts as a sensitive name in memory (EDWARD_SENSITIVE_NAMES).
# EDWARD_HOUSEHOLD=Sam,Rowan

# =============================================================================
# THIS MAC (backend/services/host_config.py)
# =============================================================================

# Where you open Edward. Links in pushes and texts use it, and so does the
# health check's public-URL probe (only when EDWARD_TUNNEL_LABEL is set).
# Behind a tunnel or proxy it is your public https URL.
# EDWARD_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000
# The /screen page (default: the base URL + /screen).
# EDWARD_PUBLIC_SCREEN_URL=http://localhost:3000/screen
# Extra browser origins allowed to call the API (comma-separated); the base
# URL's origin and the local dev origins are always allowed.
# EDWARD_CORS_ORIGINS=http://my-mac.local:3000

# The frontend's build-time values (frontend/ensure-build.sh passes them to
# `next build`; a change applies at the next build). NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL is
# the app's public origin for link previews (default: EDWARD_PUBLIC_BASE_URL,
# else http://localhost:3000). NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL is where the frontend
# server forwards /api (default http://localhost:8000); leave it unset unless
# the backend lives elsewhere -- when set, browsers also open live voice's
# WebSocket on that host directly.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://edward.example.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=

# The backend listens on every interface so devices on your network (a
# household hub) can reach it; 127.0.0.1 keeps it to this Mac.
# EDWARD_BACKEND_HOST=0.0.0.0

# LaunchAgent labels are <prefix>.backend, <prefix>.frontend ...
# (scripts/install-launchagents.sh installs them).
# EDWARD_LAUNCHD_PREFIX=com.meet-edward

# The LaunchAgent label of a tunnel in front of Edward (cloudflared, ngrok
# ...), if you run one: the health check restarts it when the public URL
# stops answering. Unset: no tunnel is checked. Tunnels give up on a request
# after about 100 s, which is why long operations answer 202 and run in the
# background.
# EDWARD_TUNNEL_LABEL=com.cloudflare.edward

# The checkout the running backend serves from (default: this one). Edward's
# own shell may never write here.
# EDWARD_LIVE_REPO=/Users/me/edward

# Where Homebrew lives (default /opt/homebrew when it exists, else
# /usr/local). The backend uses it for Edward's shell PATH and the kegs his
# shell may not write to; the scripts ask `brew --prefix`, except under
# launchd, whose agents carry the value the installer found.
# HOMEBREW_PREFIX=/opt/homebrew

# The python the nightly backup runs under (default: the real path of the
# python3.12 behind backend/.venv -- the binary to grant Full Disk Access).
# install-launchagents.sh writes it into the backup agent, so re-run the
# installer after changing it (or after brew upgrade python@3.12).
# EDWARD_FDA_PYTHON=

# =============================================================================
# DATABASE
# =============================================================================

# setup.sh creates this role and database on Homebrew's PostgreSQL 16 with
# pgvector. Tests never use it: they run on their own cluster on :5433
# (scripts/test-pg-cluster.sh, scripts/with-test-db-lock.sh). Edward's own
# shell is refused both (:5432 and :5433) and gets no DATABASE_URL.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://edward:edward@localhost:5432/edward
# Log every SQL statement (debugging only; very noisy).
# SQL_ECHO=false

# =============================================================================
# MODEL ROUTES ON THE CLAUDE API (backend/services/claude_routing.py)
# =============================================================================

# One route per purpose, each overridable with
#   EDWARD_CLAUDE_<PURPOSE>_MODEL              a claude-* id
#   EDWARD_CLAUDE_<PURPOSE>_EFFORT             low | medium | high | xhigh | max
#   EDWARD_CLAUDE_<PURPOSE>_MAX_TOKENS         1024 - 128000
#   EDWARD_CLAUDE_<PURPOSE>_THINKING_DISPLAY   summarized | omitted
#   EDWARD_CLAUDE_<PURPOSE>_CACHE_TTL          5m | 1h | off
# for PURPOSE = CHAT (your chats, ping replies, events), MIND (Edward's own
# wakes), PULSE (what the screens show), BACKGROUND (memory extraction,
# entities, consolidation, noticing), VOICE (spoken turns), JUDGE
# (evaluations). Defaults:
#   CHAT, MIND   claude-sonnet-5-5  medium  32000  summarized  5m
#   PULSE        claude-sonnet-5-5  medium  16000  summarized  5m
#   BACKGROUND   claude-haiku-5-5   high    16000  omitted     5m
#   VOICE        claude-sonnet-5-5  low      8000  summarized  1h
#   JUDGE        claude-opus-5-5    high    16000  omitted     5m
# Never run an agentic route (chat, mind) at Sonnet low. A bad value is
# ignored with one warning.
# EDWARD_CLAUDE_CHAT_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5
# EDWARD_CLAUDE_MIND_EFFORT=medium
# EDWARD_CLAUDE_BACKGROUND_CACHE_TTL=5m
# The speaker (a noticing worth saying) and the noticer; default: the mind
# route / the background route.
# EDWARD_SPEAK_MODEL=
# EDWARD_NOTICE_MODEL=
# The deadline for one Claude request, in seconds.
# CLAUDE_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300

# =============================================================================
# SPEND (backend/services/llm_spend.py, toolset/policy.py)
# =============================================================================

# A daily cap in dollars over your local day: background work stops at
# cap x share, Edward's own turns at the cap, and your own turns may run on
# into the reserve. Shown on the Fuel card and GET /api/health/spend.
# EDWARD_DAILY_SPEND_CAP_USD=5
# EDWARD_BACKGROUND_SPEND_SHARE=0.6
# EDWARD_INTERACTIVE_RESERVE_USD=3
# Optional: your prepaid credits and the local date you bought them, so the
# app can count them down. Both or neither; unset, credits are not tracked
# (console.anthropic.com is the authority either way).
# EDWARD_CREDITS_START_USD=100
# EDWARD_CREDITS_START_DATE=2026-10-01
# Per-turn caps in dollars: a turn you asked for, and any other turn.
# EDWARD_TURN_MAX_USD=3.00
# EDWARD_AUTONOMOUS_TURN_MAX_USD=1.00

# =============================================================================
# THE CODEX FALLBACK (unofficial, opt-in: only with EDWARD_LLM_PROVIDER=codex)
# =============================================================================
# Talks to the ChatGPT backend with the Codex CLI's own login (`codex login`
# writes ~/.codex/auth.json). That is not an official API for third-party
# apps and may break or conflict with OpenAI's terms; use it at your own
# risk. None of these apply on the Claude API.
# The login, or an OPENAI_API_KEY used as the credential in its place, and
# the CLI's model catalog (model ids come from it).
# CODEX_AUTH_PATH=~/.codex/auth.json
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# CODEX_MODELS_CACHE_PATH=~/.codex/models_cache.json
# CODEX_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300
# Models and reasoning efforts per purpose. Unset: the mind runs on the
# app's model setting, the pulse on gpt-5.6-terra when the catalog lists
# it, and chat and background work at the model's own effort.
# EDWARD_BACKGROUND_MODEL=gpt-5.6-terra
# EDWARD_VOICE_MODEL=gpt-5.6-luna
# EDWARD_ENTITY_MODEL=gpt-5.6-luna
# EDWARD_MIND_MODEL=
# EDWARD_PULSE_MODEL=gpt-5.6-terra
# EDWARD_CHAT_REASONING_EFFORT=
# EDWARD_BACKGROUND_REASONING_EFFORT=
# EDWARD_MIND_REASONING_EFFORT=high
# EDWARD_PULSE_REASONING_EFFORT=low
# Per wake reason, overriding the mind's effort ("*" = every other reason).
# EDWARD_MIND_EFFORT_MAP=mention=medium,*=high

# =============================================================================
# TRUST AT THE BOUNDARY (docs/architecture/trust-boundary.md)
# =============================================================================

# Secrets are scanned out of everything that leaves (commands, sends,
# fetched URLs, typing). off is an emergency switch only.
# EDWARD_EGRESS_SECRET_SCAN=on
# Hold outward calls from turns that read untrusted content for your
# Approve/Deny: off | shadow (log what would be held) | on.
# EDWARD_TAINT_APPROVALS=off

# =============================================================================
# THE TOOLSET AND EDWARD'S COMPUTER (docs/architecture/computer.md)
# =============================================================================

# v2 = the 14-tool set on every surface; legacy = the old registry
# (a rollback switch).
# EDWARD_TOOLSET=v2
# EDWARD_MAX_TOOL_ROUNDS=60
# Native bash/editor tools under Claude (off = custom schemas), and
# Anthropic's server-side web search/fetch (off = Brave + a local fetch).
# EDWARD_NATIVE_COMPUTER_TOOLS=on
# EDWARD_SERVER_WEB_TOOLS=on
# The web search's country (a two-letter code).
# EDWARD_SEARCH_COUNTRY=US

# Edward's own home directory and his sandboxed shell (Seatbelt). Turning
# the sandbox off is an emergency switch: every command is then logged.
# What the sandbox is: it keeps his shell off Edward's checkout, his own
# keys, Postgres' files and the python/postgres installs, Edward's API and
# databases and the WhatsApp bridge. What it isn't: a wall. The shell runs
# as your macOS user and can write the rest of your home (shell startup
# files, ~/Library/LaunchAgents) and Homebrew, where programs run outside
# the sandbox. For strong isolation, run Edward as a separate macOS user
# (docs/ADAPTING.md, prompt G; docs/architecture/computer.md).
# EDWARD_HOME=~/edward-home
# EDWARD_COMPUTER_SANDBOX=on
# EDWARD_SHELL_TIMEOUT_S=180          # per command
# EDWARD_SHELL_MAX_RSS_MB=2048
# EDWARD_SHELL_MAX_SESSIONS=4
# EDWARD_SHELL_MIN_FREE_GB=5
# Where Playwright's headless shell is (Edward's own invisible browser;
# setup.sh installs it at Playwright's default, ~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright).
# PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH=

# Your calendar and reminders, read from their own databases (needs Full
# Disk Access for the backend's python; off = apple-mcp only), and the
# EventKit helper setup.sh builds for the writes you ask for (grant it once
# with: bash backend/scripts/eventkit_request_access.sh, at the Mac).
# EDWARD_CALENDAR_SQLITE=on
# EDWARD_CALENDAR_DB='~/Library/Group Containers/group.com.apple.calendar/Calendar.sqlitedb'
# EDWARD_REMINDERS_SQLITE=on
# EDWARD_REMINDERS_DIR='~/Library/Group Containers/group.com.apple.reminders/Container_v1/Stores'
# EDWARD_EVENTKIT=~/edward-eventkit/bin/edward-eventkit

# Releases Edward's shell may never write to, and the dormant coding
# sessions: where Codex may run, and the worktrees it must never touch.
# EDWARD_RELEASES_DIR=~/edward-releases
# EDWARD_CODEX_SCRATCH_ROOT=~/edward-scratch/codex
# EDWARD_CODEX_WORKTREE_ROOTS=~/edward-evolve
# EDWARD_HUMAN_WORKTREE_ROOTS=~/edward-worktrees

# =============================================================================
# EDWARD'S HANDS IN YOUR CHROME (chrome-extension/)
# =============================================================================

# Off by default: nothing launches, raises or drives your Chrome. On, Edward
# can use your own Chrome through the Edward extension (load chrome-extension/
# unpacked) and raise the /screen page as a Chrome tab. /screen still works
# when you open it by hand, and Edward's own invisible browser (Playwright's
# headless shell) works either way.
# EDWARD_CHROME_HANDS=off
# Launch Chrome with --silent-debugger-extension-api, which hides Chrome's
# "being debugged" bar. Only matters with the hands on.
# EDWARD_CHROME_SILENT_DEBUGGER=off
# The extension's socket (ws://127.0.0.1:8000/api/browser/ext) takes only a
# direct connection from this Mac -- no proxy or tunnel headers -- whose
# Origin is chrome-extension://<id> for Edward's own extension. Its manifest
# carries a fixed public key, so every unpacked copy gets the same id,
# appfgnpjahkedldopocalbkoommcalae, and by default only that id is accepted;
# a refused socket's log line names the id it offered. Set this to accept
# other ids instead (comma-separated; it replaces the default), e.g. for a
# fork that changes or removes the manifest's key. The key is public, so an
# extension built on purpose with the same key gets the same id: the pin
# keeps out your other extensions, not a deliberate copy. And a local
# program can forge every header: the socket keeps out the network, the
# tunnel, web pages and other extensions, not this Mac's own processes.
# EDWARD_CHROME_EXTENSION_ID=

# =============================================================================
# PINGS AND MEDIA (iMessage; docs/architecture/pings.md, media.md)
# =============================================================================

# Pings need iMessage reading on: Settings > Listening in the app (off by
# default; the backend's python needs Full Disk Access to read chat.db), and
# your handles in EDWARD_OWNER_HANDLES.
# @Edward in any chat, your self-chat, or a reply to Edward's message then
# gets an answer in its own turn. off = the runner stops and the mind's
# mention wake answers @Edward instead.
# EDWARD_PINGS=on
# EDWARD_PING_TURN_TIMEOUT_S=600
# EDWARD_PING_CONCURRENCY=2
# EDWARD_PING_FRIEND_TURNS_PER_HOUR=6     # friends' ping turns per chat
# A friend's ping (anyone but you) gets no shell, no file editor, none of
# your Chrome, no screens, and `look` only at what was sent in a chat, a
# link or Edward's own browser: their message is someone else's text, and
# the shell runs as your macOS user. on = friends get the full toolset (the
# original install's choice), at that risk. Your own pings keep everything.
# EDWARD_FRIENDS_FULL_TOOLSET=off
# Photos, PDFs, voice messages and videos read for real (off = placeholders;
# voice messages are transcribed with ELEVENLABS_API_KEY), and the Swift
# media helper setup.sh builds.
# EDWARD_MEDIA=on
# EDWARD_MEDIATOOL=~/edward-media/bin/edward-mediatool
# Where Edward stages files it sends over iMessage.
# EDWARD_IMESSAGE_OUTBOX=~/Library/Messages/Attachments/Edward

# =============================================================================
# NOTICING, REACHING YOU AND QUIET HOURS (docs/architecture/noticing.md)
# =============================================================================

# off: messages are stored, pings answered, promises kept, nothing noticed.
# shadow: notice and decide, never speak. notice: say what matters, once.
# EDWARD_PROACTIVITY=off
# Quiet hours, local, "start-end" in whole hours 0-23 (wraps past midnight;
# equal hours mean none): Edward's own pushes become notes on Today (care
# alerts excepted) and nothing is said aloud on the screens. A bad value
# falls back to 22-7.
# EDWARD_QUIET_HOURS=22-7
# floor: only travel or household matters closing within 3 h may push;
# gate: the gate's per-category ladder decides.
# EDWARD_NOTICE_PUSH=floor
# Speak turns a day (and at most one per chat an hour).
# EDWARD_SPEAK_BUDGET=8
# A daily cap on Edward's own pushes (unset: none).
# EDWARD_PUSH_BUDGET=
# How an urgent reach gets to you: push | imessage_self (a text to your
# first handle) | edward_number (SMS from Edward's Twilio number). Any
# failure falls back to a push.
# EDWARD_OWNER_CHANNEL=push
# The late-dose alert for timed care items on the hub (off = kill switch).
# EDWARD_CARE_WATCH=on

# =============================================================================
# THE HOUSEHOLD HUB AND THE PHOTO DOOR (/hub, /photos; docs/architecture/hub.md)
# =============================================================================

# Your pets and their care, planted once into an empty board. Copy
# backend/hub_seed.example.json to backend/hub_seed.json (git ignores it),
# describe your own, and point this at it (relative paths are read from
# backend/). Unset: the board starts empty. The hub's people are
# EDWARD_HOUSEHOLD, above.
# EDWARD_HUB_SEED=hub_seed.json
# Photos for the hub's screensaver, and their thumbnail cache.
# EDWARD_PHOTOS_DIR=~/edward-photos
# EDWARD_PHOTO_THUMBS_DIR=~/Library/Caches/edward-photo-thumbs
# A second, photos-only login (the "photo door", /photos): a bcrypt hash of
# its password, in single quotes. Unset or malformed: the door is off.
# Make the hash from the repo root, after ./setup.sh (bcrypt is in the
# backend's venv, not in macOS's python3):
#   backend/.venv/bin/python -c "import bcrypt, getpass; print(bcrypt.hashpw(getpass.getpass().encode(), bcrypt.gensalt()).decode())"
# PHOTO_DOOR_PASSWORD_HASH='$2b$12$...'

# =============================================================================
# MESSAGING AND INTEGRATIONS (docs/architecture/integrations.md)
# =============================================================================

# Sending iMessages through Messages.app (AppleScript). Reading them is the
# Listening switch in the app (see PINGS above).
# IMESSAGE_ENABLED=true

# apple-mcp (Calendar, Reminders, Notes, Mail, Contacts, Maps, Messages):
# install bun, clone jxnl/apple-mcp, `bun install` it, and point these at it.
# MCP_APPLE_ENABLED=false
# MCP_APPLE_DIR=~/apple-mcp
# BUN_PATH=~/.bun/bin/bun

# WhatsApp through the whatsapp-mcp project's Go bridge, which you run
# yourself (its own LaunchAgent or a terminal) and pair once with the QR in
# its log; Edward talks to its MCP server.
# MCP_WHATSAPP_ENABLED=false
# MCP_WHATSAPP_SERVER_DIR=~/whatsapp-mcp/whatsapp-mcp-server
# MCP_WHATSAPP_BRIDGE_LOG=/tmp/whatsapp-bridge.log

# Every MCP call is bounded, in seconds.
# MCP_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# Twilio: an SMS/WhatsApp number of Edward's own. The webhooks must be
# reachable from the internet (a tunnel and EDWARD_PUBLIC_BASE_URL), and they
# fail closed: without TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN, or with a bad X-Twilio-Signature
# for the URL below (or the URL as received through a proxy), every inbound
# request is refused.
# Only you get an answer: a text from one of EDWARD_OWNER_HANDLES gets a
# turn, so put your mobile number there (with its country code), or nobody
# is answered. Anyone else's text is held: no turn, no reply, no push; the
# backend log names the sender, and the ledger (ledger_events kind
# twilio_inbound, outcome held) keeps the text for you.
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=AC...
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER=+15555550123
# TWILIO_WEBHOOK_URL=https://edward.example.com/api/webhook/twilio
# TWILIO_WHATSAPP_WEBHOOK_URL=https://edward.example.com/api/webhook/twilio/whatsapp
# TWILIO_RATE_LIMIT=10                 # inbound messages per sender per window
# TWILIO_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW=60          # seconds
# Answer texts from anyone, not just you. RISK: a turn can read what Edward
# reads (your calendar, memories, messages, files) and its answer is texted
# back to the sender, so anyone who learns the number can try to talk him
# into sending them your data. Their turns are told the sender is not you
# and to share nothing of yours -- a prompt, not a wall. Off by default.
# EDWARD_TWILIO_ANSWER_ANYONE=off

# Gmail: an OAuth client from Google Cloud (a "Web application" client with
# the redirect below), either as two values or as the downloaded JSON; then
# connect it in the app (Settings > Gmail). Behind a public URL the redirect
# is https://<your host>/api/gmail/oauth/callback.
# GMAIL_CLIENT_ID=
# GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRETS_FILE=
# GMAIL_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/gmail/oauth/callback
# GMAIL_ACCOUNT=sam@example.com

# Mail.app's Envelope Index, read by the mail listener (needs Full Disk
# Access); set it when your macOS keeps Mail's data in another V folder.
# EDWARD_MAIL_ENVELOPE_INDEX='~/Library/Mail/V10/MailData/Envelope Index'

# An Alexa skill. Leave it unset unless you use one: POST /api/alexa is
# public and trusts any request that carries this id (there is no Amazon
# signature check).
# ALEXA_SKILL_ID=

# Home Assistant, for announcements and sounds on Echo devices.
# HOME_ASSISTANT_URL=http://localhost:8123
# HOME_ASSISTANT_TOKEN=
# HOME_ASSISTANT_ECHO_PREFIX=media_player.echo
# HOME_ASSISTANT_DEFAULT_DEVICE=media_player.echo_dot   # default: <prefix>_dot

# Brave Search: the web search when the server tools are off and on the
# Codex fallback (the local page fetch needs no key).
# BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY=

# A hosted-page service (the legacy toolset's "publish a page" tools). Both
# unset: not configured. See services/html_hosting_service.py for the API it
# expects.
# HTML_HOSTING_URL=
# HTML_HOSTING_API_KEY=

# =============================================================================
# VOICE (ElevenLabs: Scribe for listening, TTS for speaking)
# =============================================================================

# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=
# ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID=21m00Tcm4TlvDq8ikWAM
# ELEVENLABS_TTS_MODEL=eleven_flash_v2_5

# =============================================================================
# PUSH NOTIFICATIONS (Web Push)
# =============================================================================

# Generate a pair once:  npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Who push services may contact about this sender: an address, or a
# mailto:/https: URL. Default mailto:admin@localhost.
# VAPID_CONTACT_EMAIL=you@example.com

# =============================================================================
# SIGN-IN AND FILES
# =============================================================================

# Until a password is set, the API answers only the sign-in pages (and
# /health). The first password needs a one-time setup code: while no
# password exists, the backend keeps it in backend/.setup_code (made when
# the backend starts, readable by your user alone, deleted once a password
# is set; the log and the scripts say where it is, never what it is). On
# this Mac, from the checkout:
#   cat backend/.setup_code
# then open http://localhost:3000 on this Mac and type it in with your new
# password. The request must also come as JSON from a page at localhost on
# this Mac (the login page does that), so a phone or another computer on
# your network is told to use the Mac -- but those checks read headers a
# device on your network can forge; the code is what keeps the install
# yours. From a Mac with no screen: ssh -L 3000:localhost:3000
# you@my-mac.local, open localhost:3000 on your own computer, and read the
# code over ssh. 10 wrong codes in 15 minutes pause setup; they never count
# against signing in.

# The session-token secret. Unset: one is generated and kept in
# backend/.jwt_secret (survives restarts; git ignores it). Whoever holds it
# can mint your session, so Edward's shell and editor can't read that file
# (nor .env, nor the setup code), and his shell can't connect to ports 8000
# or 3000, the WhatsApp bridge or the databases. The secret scan also
# redacts the key from his tool output and blocks it from leaving, whether
# it comes from here or the file. Prefer leaving it unset: a value here is
# in the backend's environment, which the programs it starts inherit.
# JWT_SECRET_KEY=

# Sign-in allows 5 failed tries per client in 15 minutes, and 100 from all
# clients together (past that, sign-in and the photo door pause for everyone
# until the oldest failures age out; signed-in sessions are unaffected, and
# changing the password is limited per session, 5 in 15 minutes, never by
# that ceiling). Every browser that reaches Edward through the frontend
# without a tunnel, this Mac's own included, counts as one client. Behind a
# tunnel the client is read from the tunnel's own headers, which only counts
# when the request reached the backend from this Mac: leave uvicorn's
# FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS unset (or loopback only).

# Session cookies carry Secure, which browsers accept from https:// and
# http://localhost. off only for a plain-HTTP network setup you trust; a hub
# kiosk can instead start its browser with
# --unsafely-treat-insecure-origin-as-secure=http://my-mac.local:3000
# EDWARD_COOKIE_SECURE=on
# Uploaded and generated files (relative to backend/).
# FILE_STORAGE_ROOT=./storage

# =============================================================================
# MEMORY (docs/architecture/memory.md)
# =============================================================================

# Names whose mention marks a memory sensitive -- held for you, never
# retired by decay or a contradiction verdict: pets, family, anyone whose
# mentions should be kept. Comma-separated, matched as whole words; the
# household (EDWARD_HOUSEHOLD) other than you is included already.
# EDWARD_SENSITIVE_NAMES=Biscuit,Maple
# One-off instructions that are not your own lapse after 30 days. Unset:
# every such instruction. An install upgraded from an older Edward sets its
# upgrade day (an ISO date) so the backlog written before provenance existed
# is kept. Read at startup.
# EDWARD_INSTRUCTION_HORIZON_SINCE=
# The knowledge graph (Graphiti + Kuzu) in its own worker:
#   bash scripts/install-launchagents.sh --with-graph
# It takes both switches. GRAPHITI_ENABLED queues every new memory and lets
# the worker start; GRAPHITI_INGEST_ENABLED lets the worker drain that queue
# into the graph. With only the first, the graph stays empty and the queue
# only grows. Each memory takes the worker 80-130 s and a few calls on the
# background route (inside the daily spend cap). POST
# /api/memory/graph/backfill queues the memories you already have.
# GRAPHITI_ENABLED=false
# GRAPHITI_INGEST_ENABLED=false
# GRAPHITI_DB_PATH=./storage/graphiti.kuzu
# GRAPH_WORKER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8001

# =============================================================================
# BACKUPS AND HEALTH CHECKS (docs/architecture/ops-health-backups.md)
# =============================================================================

# Nightly pg_dump + graph copy (03:30), verified and rotated, readable by
# you alone (the folder 700, each dump 600). A path under /Volumes/ is a
# removable drive: the backup refuses to run while it is unplugged, and
# needs Full Disk Access for its python (EDWARD_FDA_PYTHON).
# EDWARD_BACKUP_DIR=~/edward-backups
# EDWARD_BACKUP_KEEP=14                  # daily dumps kept
# EDWARD_BACKUP_KEEP_KUZU=3              # graph copies kept
# The Postgres role the backup connects as (default: $PGUSER, else your
# macOS user -- Homebrew's superuser), and the database it dumps.
# EDWARD_BACKUP_USER=
# EDWARD_DB_NAME=edward

# The watchdog (scripts/health_check.sh, every 5 min + a deep check at 08:00).
# Its state and log, and the file the scheduler stamps every poll.
# EDWARD_HEALTH_STATE=~/.edward-health
# EDWARD_SCHEDULER_ALIVE_PATH=~/.edward-health/scheduler.alive
# A program run with two arguments (title, body) when something needs a
# human, besides the macOS notification.
# EDWARD_HEALTH_NOTIFY_CMD=
# Postgres's binaries, data directory and log, if not Homebrew's postgresql@16
# (defaults under $(brew --prefix): opt/postgresql@16/bin,
# var/postgresql@16, var/log/postgresql@16.log). EDWARD_PG_BIN also goes
# on the PATH of backend/start.sh, the graph worker and the LaunchAgents,
# right after the system's own directories (postgresql@16 is keg-only, so
# Homebrew puts no psql or pg_config on PATH). Edward's shell may not write
# EDWARD_PG_BIN or EDWARD_PGDATA.
# EDWARD_PG_BIN=/opt/homebrew/opt/postgresql@16/bin
# EDWARD_PGDATA=/opt/homebrew/var/postgresql@16
# EDWARD_PG_LOG=/opt/homebrew/var/log/postgresql@16.log
# Deep checks that only make sense on some setups (any value turns them on):
# a hub kiosk that polls /api/hub/* all day, and mail flowing.
# EDWARD_HEALTH_HUB_KIOSK=
# EDWARD_HEALTH_EMAIL=

# =============================================================================
# TESTS AND TOOLING ONLY -- never in this file, never on the running backend
# =============================================================================
# Set these in the shell of a test run or a tool. Several replace real
# behaviour with fakes (embeddings, dry-run sends) and would quietly break a
# live Edward.
# The test database, on its own cluster (scripts/test-pg-cluster.sh: its
# data directory and its Postgres binaries, default postgresql@16's). It
# trusts your macOS user as its superuser on localhost, so Edward's shell
# may neither connect to it nor write its data directory.
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://edward:edward@localhost:5433/edward_test
# EDWARD_TEST_PGDATA=~/.edward-test-pg
# EDWARD_TEST_PG_BIN=
# Deterministic fake embeddings (tests, evals and spawned coding runs).
# EDWARD_FAKE_EMBEDDINGS=1
# Runs the one headless-browser integration test.
# EDWARD_TEST_BROWSER=1
# Every outward call becomes a recorded dry run (the judgement check).
# EDWARD_DRY_RUN_OUTWARD=1
# The python the UX harness runs its mock API with
# (frontend/scripts/mock-api/run-harness.sh; default backend/.venv's).
# EDWARD_HARNESS_PYTHON=
# The frontend build id a deploy hands to `next build`.
# EDWARD_BUILD_ID=
